window.bookSummaryJSON = "<p><b><i>Clover Bradley was having the worst day of her life and then she got kidnapped</i></b>. <i>In her own home</i>. Well, her empty and currently being renovated childhood home on the edge of Disciple, West Virginia and not her former cozy cottage on the grounds of the Dixie Yonder Hotel where she <i>was</i> working and thriving until that very morning. But it still counts and definitely makes her very bad day much worse.<br> <br> But that’s not even the crazy part. The crazy part is that she’s falling for her kidnapper. A handsome man, if a little morally challenged, who is probably very dangerous and has threatened to kill her half a dozen times over the span of just one day, but what can one do? The attraction is there.<br> <br> Riggs is just trying his best to stay out of prison. Which seems at odds with his current actions, but need-to-know nuances make all these nonsensical pieces fit. And even though Clover isn’t a model captive—she’s a complainer of the princess variety—he finds himself getting attached. If he hadn’t been forced to kidnap her to save his own hide, he could see a future where the two of them end up together.<br> <br> So he figures… why not save her life by endangering her some more?<br> <br> And she figures… well, it’s better than being left to rot in her own basement.<br> <br> And that’s, of course, when things get interesting.<br> <br> <b><i>The Comfort in the Brave is an ironic twist of fate inbred to a really bad decision. It’s a captive romance turned ride-or-die in a crazy escape from Blackberry Hill—but this time Collin Creed isn’t coming to save anyone, he’s looking for revenge. </i></b><br> <br> <b>TROPES</b><br> Falling for the Enemy<br> Ride or Die<br> Haughty Princess<br> Redemptive Anti-Hero<br> Morally Grey<br> Found Family</p>"; 
